    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the only integer whose square is less than its double, we need to solve the inequality \\(x^2 < 2x\\).\n\nFirst, rearrange the inequality:\n\\[x^2 - 2x < 0.\\]\n\nNext, factor the left-hand side:\n\\[x(x - 2) < 0.\\]\n\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine where the product \\(x(x - 2)\\) is negative. The critical points are \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\), which divide the number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, 0)\\), \\((0, 2)\\), and \\((2, \\infty)\\).\n\nWe test a point from each interval to determine the sign of the product \\(x(x - 2)\\):\n\n1. For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, 0)\\), choose \\(x = -1\\):\n   \\[(-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 > 0.\\]\n   So, the product is positive in this interval.\n\n2. For \\(x \\in (0, 2)\\), choose \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[(1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 < 0.\\]\n   So, the product is negative in this interval.\n\n3. For \\(x \\in (2, \\infty)\\), choose \\(x = 3\\):\n   \\[(3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 > 0.\\]\n   So, the product is positive in this interval.\n\nFrom this analysis, we see that \\(x(x - 2)\\) is negative in the interval \\((0, 2)\\). Therefore, the solution to the inequality \\(x(x - 2) < 0\\) is:\n\\[0 < x < 2.\\]\n\nThe only integer in this interval is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nThus, the only integer whose square is less than its double is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
